<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Finland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Finnish</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Finland is a country in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. It was an integral part of the Swedish realm for centuries and supplied troops to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> lost Finland to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1809, after which it became a grand duchy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/finland.jpg" alt="Finland" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Finland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Birger Jarl, who governed for his son King Valdemar from 1250 until 1266, strengthened the crown, issued peace laws, founded Stockholm, first mentioned in 1252, and led an expedition, traditionally dated to 1249, that brought Tavastia in <b>Finland</b> under Swedish rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The parliamentary reform of 1906 made <b>Finland</b> the first country in Europe where women could both vote and stand for parliament; nineteen women were elected in 1907.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Grain from the estates of Harria and Vironia was another export, and the town also traded with <b>Finland</b> and Sweden across the Gulf of Finland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In March 1918 the government moved the capital from Petrograd to Moscow and signed the Treaty of Brest-Litovsk with the Central Powers, giving up Poland, the Baltic lands, <b>Finland</b> and Ukraine.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Civil War of 1918–1921 the Red Army, organised by Leon Trotsky, defeated the White armies and foreign intervention forces, and Soviet republics were established or restored by force in Ukraine, Belarus and the Transcaucasus, while <b>Finland</b>, Poland and the Baltic states secured their independence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>At its height between about 1660 and 1700, Sweden controlled the Baltic from <b>Finland</b> and Estonia in the east to Pomerania and Wismar in the south.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
